<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magnus VI of Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1238">1238</a>–1280</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Magnus the Lawmender, Magnus Lagabøte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magnus VI, called the Lawmender, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1263 to 1280. He gave Norway a single national law code, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-lagabotes-land-law">Landslov of 1274</a>, and a town law in 1276, replacing the regional laws. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-perth">Treaty of Perth</a> in 1266 he ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and the Isle of Man to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> while keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magnus-vi-of-norway.jpg" alt="Magnus VI of Norway" width="197" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bugge (1870–1929) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magnus_Haakonsson_(Stavanger_cathedral).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Magnus Haakonsson was born in Tunsberg and baptised in May 1238, the youngest son of King Håkon Håkonsson and Margaret Skulesdatter. He grew up mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>. In 1257 his older brother Håkon died, leaving Magnus heir to the kingdom, and his father gave him the title of king the same year. On 11 September 1261 he married Ingeborg, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-iv-of-denmark">Eric IV of Denmark</a>, Håkon died on 16 December 1263 during a campaign against the Scottish king over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a>, and Magnus became ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Magnus abandoned his father's aggressive foreign policy. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-perth">Treaty of Perth</a> in 1266 he gave up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and the Isle of Man to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> for a large sum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and a yearly payment, while the Scots recognised Norwegian rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>. In the 1260s the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> was officially defined for the first time. In 1275 he gathered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leidang">leidang</a> fleet, for the only time in his reign, when the deposed Swedish king Valdemar Birgersson fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, but he failed to reconcile Valdemar's brothers and withdrew without fighting.</p><p>His main work was the law. Helped by legal scholars and advisers, he had a modernised code adopted at the Things in 1274 (the Landslov), applying to the whole country including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> and replacing the regional laws, and a town law (the Bylov) in 1276. The code treated crime as an offence against the state rather than the individual, narrowed personal vengeance and made the throne the source of justice. In 1273 he gave his five-year-old son Eric the title of king and his brother Håkon that of duke, settling the succession. The Tønsberg Concord of 1277 with Archbishop Jon Raude of Nidaros confirmed certain privileges of the clergy but ended the Church's claim that the kingdom was its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Magnus fell ill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in the spring of 1280, planned to have Eric crowned co-ruler at midsummer, and died on 9 May. Eric succeeded at the age of 12, with real power in the hands of advisers, among them the widow Ingeborg. Magnus was buried in the Franciscan monastery church in Bergen, later Bergen Cathedral. He was remembered as a good ruler who ruled by law rather than by the sword, and he is mostly considered wise for sparing the kingdom needless wars. His saga, commissioned from Sturla Þórðarson, survives only as a short fragment.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Magnus the Lawmender” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Haakon IV (r. 1217–1263) built a royal administration of district officials (sysselmenn) and chancery clerks, and his son <b>Magnus VI</b>, called the Lawmender, issued a national law code (Landsloven) in 1274 and a town law soon after, among the earliest codes in Europe valid for a whole kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tønsberg, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.3,10.42&amp;year=1265" aria-label="Tønsberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4770 171 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Magnus VI of Norway"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5220 441h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5220 441h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.3,10.42&amp;year=1265">What was here in 1265? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
